    {
      "parameters": {
        "promptType": "define",
        "text": "=Analysis: {{ JSON.stringify($json.data) }}",
        "hasOutputParser": true,
        "options": {
          "systemMessage": "=You will receive ONE bundle that contains an array called **analysis**.\nEach element has:\n- company = domain or name\n- analysis_linkedin = a ```json``` string that, once un-escaped, becomes\n  valid JSON with the keys\n  - current_positioning_and_message\n  - recent_launches\n  - recent_major_product_releases\n  - recent_funding_or_customer_announcements\n\nTASK\nProduce a single Gmail-ready Markdown document titled:\n\n# Weekly Competitive Digest\n\nFor every object in **analysis** (in the order provided) output:\n\n**Company Name**\n🪄 Positioning\n- current_positioning_and_message\n\n🚀 Recent Launches\n- each item in recent_launches\n- each item in recent_major_product_releases\n  (merge lists; omit this whole section if nothing to show)\n\n💰 Recent Fund-raising or Customer Announcements\n- each item in recent_funding_or_customer_announcements\n  (omit this section if the list is empty or reads\n  \"No public information found\")\n\nFORMATTING RULES\n1. Produce ONE Markdown document—no arrays, no extra prose.\n2. Use “. ” (bullet + space) for every list item.\n3. Remove any header whose list would be empty.\n4. Preserve dates / figures exactly as in the source.\n5. Do **not** add commentary, summaries, or extra sections.\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT\nReturn exactly one JSON object with a single key\n- email_markdown, whose value is the full Markdown string.\n\n"
        }
      },
